      <div class=3D"card-header">
        <div class=3D"card-title">Why Intertek for Valuation?</div>
        <div class=3D"card-subtitle">Company Selection Rationale</div>
      </div>
      <ul class=3D"bullets">
        <li><strong>Underused in interviews</strong> =E2=80=94 Intertek is =
rarely cited vs. overused names (Apple, LVMH, Tesla). Immediately signals s=
ector depth.</li>
        <li><strong>Clean, defendable financials</strong> =E2=80=94 5+ year=
s of consistent reported data on LSE, clean IFRS, stable margins making ass=
umptions tractable.</li>
        <li><strong>Excellent comparable set</strong> =E2=80=94 Bureau Veri=
tas, SGS, Element, Eurofins, MISTRAS create a well-defined peer group.</li>
        <li><strong>Realistic acquisition target</strong> =E2=80=94 Market =
cap ~=C2=A35bn. Acquirable by Danaher, Bureau Veritas, SGS, or a PE consort=
ium.</li>
        <li><strong>Asset-light with strong FCF</strong> =E2=80=94 Ideal fo=
r both DCF and LBO modelling. High ROIC, low CapEx relative to revenue (~3.=
5%).</li>
        <li><strong>Global niche moat</strong> =E2=80=94 Regulation-driven =
demand ensures predictable revenue. Excellent for explaining competitive dy=
namics.</li>
        <li><strong>Strategic premium potential</strong> =E2=80=94 Acquirer=
 synergies (geographic overlap, lab consolidation, pricing power) are quant=
ifiable and compelling.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

