<!-- =3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D PART 6 =
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D -->
<div class=3D"section" id=3D"p6">
  <div class=3D"section-badge">Part VI</div>
  <div class=3D"section-header">Terminal Value Analysis</div>
  <div class=3D"section-desc">Two methodologies for terminal value estimati=
on with cross-validation. All =C2=A3m.</div>

  <div class=3D"grid-2">
    <div class=3D"card">
      <div class=3D"card-header"><div class=3D"card-title">Method 1: Gordon=
 Growth Model</div><div class=3D"card-subtitle">Perpetual Growth / Perpetui=
ty</div></div>
      <div class=3D"formula-box">
Terminal Value (GGM) =3D FCF=E2=82=82=E2=82=80=E2=82=82=E2=82=89 =C3=97 (1 =
+ g) / (WACC =E2=80=93 g)

Inputs:
  FCF=E2=82=82=E2=82=80=E2=82=82=E2=82=89      =3D =C2=A3502m
  Growth Rate g =3D 2.5%   (nominal, perpetual)
  WACC          =3D 8.5%

Calculation:
  TV =3D 502 =C3=97 (1.025) / (0.085 =E2=80=93 0.025)
  TV =3D 514.6 / 0.060
  TV =3D =C2=A38,577m (undiscounted)

  PV of Terminal Value =3D 8,577 / (1.085)^5
  PV of TV             =3D 8,577 / 1.504
  PV of TV             =3D =C2=A35,703m
      </div>
      <h4>Growth Rate Justification (g =3D 2.5%)</h4>
      <ul class=3D"bullets">
        <li>UK nominal GDP growth long-run: ~2.5=E2=80=933.0% (BoE consensu=
s)</li>
        <li>Intertek's terminal growth is anchored to global nominal GDP, n=
ot UK specifically (70%+ revenues outside UK)</li>
        <li>2.5% is conservative: TIC sector secular tailwinds (ESG, digita=
lisation, emerging markets) could sustain 3.0=E2=80=933.5% longer-term</li>
        <li>A business cannot grow faster than the economy in perpetuity =
=E2=80=94 this rate is economically defensible</li>
        <li>Sensitivity: 2.0=E2=80=933.0% range tested (see Part X)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class=3D"card">
      <div class=3D"card-header"><div class=3D"card-title">Method 2: Exit M=
ultiple Method</div><div class=3D"card-subtitle">EV / EBITDA Exit</div></di=
v>
      <div class=3D"formula-box">
Terminal Value (EM) =3D EBITDA=E2=82=82=E2=82=80=E2=82=82=E2=82=89 =C3=97 E=
xit Multiple

Inputs:
  EBITDA=E2=82=82=E2=82=80=E2=82=82=E2=82=89    =3D =C2=A3803m
  Exit Multiple  =3D 13.0=C3=97 (sector median NTM EV/EBITDA)
  Multiple range =3D 12.0=E2=80=9314.0=C3=97

Calculation (Base =E2=80=93 13=C3=97):
  TV =3D 803 =C3=97 13.0 =3D =C2=A310,439m (undiscounted)

  PV of Terminal Value =3D 10,439 / (1.085)^5
  PV of TV             =3D 10,439 / 1.504
  PV of TV             =3D =C2=A36,941m

Cross-check (12=C3=97): PV =3D =C2=A36,406m
Cross-check (14=C3=97): PV =3D =C2=A37,475m
      </div>
      <h4>Exit Multiple Justification (13=C3=97)</h4>
      <ul class=3D"bullets">
        <li>Current Intertek trading: 11.8=C3=97 =E2=80=94 our exit multipl=
e assumes a <em>modest</em> re-rating to sector median over 5Y</li>
        <li>Sector NTM median (SGS, BV, Eurofins): 13.5=C3=97 EV/EBITDA =E2=
=80=94 13.0=C3=97 is conservative</li>
        <li>TIC sector has traded at 13=E2=80=9316=C3=97 through-cycle; 13=
=C3=97 at terminal represents mid-cycle, not peak</li>
        <li>No multiple expansion required =E2=80=94 this is a "regression =
to sector mean" assumption, not bullish</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class=3D"card">
    <div class=3D"card-header"><div class=3D"card-title">Terminal Value Rec=
onciliation</div></div>
    <table class=3D"data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>TV Undiscounted</th><th>PV of TV</th><t=
h>% of Total EV</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Gordon Growth Model (g=3D2.5%)</td><td>=C2=A38,577m</td><td=
>=C2=A35,703m</td><td>75.9%</td><td>Conservative</td></tr>
        <tr class=3D"highlight"><td>Exit Multiple (13=C3=97 EBITDA)</td><td=
>=C2=A310,439m</td><td>=C2=A36,941m</td><td>79.3%</td><td>Market-anchored</=
td></tr>
        <tr><td>GGM Implied Exit Multiple</td><td colspan=3D"2">8,577 / 803=
 =3D <strong>10.7=C3=97</strong></td><td colspan=3D"2" class=3D"negative">=
=E2=86=93 Below current market =E2=80=94 conservative anchor</td></tr>
        <tr class=3D"total"><td>Blended TV (50/50)</td><td>=C2=A39,508m</td=
><td>=C2=A36,322m</td><td>77.8%</td><td>Base case</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class=3D"navy-box" style=3D"margin-top:1rem;">
      <strong>Key Insight:</strong> The GGM implies an exit multiple of onl=
y 10.7=C3=97 EBITDA =E2=80=94 below the current trading multiple of 11.8=C3=
=97. This is internally consistent but overly pessimistic given sector stru=
ctural growth. The exit multiple method at 13=C3=97 is more defensible. In =
practice, the GGM serves as a floor and the exit multiple as a ceiling, wit=
h a blended average used for the central valuation. The high TV as a % of t=
otal EV (76=E2=80=9379%) is normal for a high-quality, low-CapEx business =
=E2=80=94 this is a feature, not a flaw, reflecting Intertek's long-duratio=
n earnings power.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

