    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">1. Walk me through your DCF.=
 What are the three key drivers of value?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">The thre=
e key drivers in my Intertek DCF are: (1) Revenue growth =E2=80=94 I've mod=
elled 5.5% in 2025E declining to 4.2% by 2029E, anchored to TIC sector stru=
ctural growth (ESG, EV testing, food safety), consistent with but slightly =
below Intertek's 5-year historical organic CAGR of 5.7%; (2) EBITDA margins=
 =E2=80=94 held at 19.0=E2=80=9320.0%, expanding modestly through operating=
 leverage on the fixed-cost laboratory base, and (3) WACC at 8.5%, derived =
from CAPM with a levered beta of 0.82, risk-free rate of 4.15% (UK 10Y Gilt=
), and ERP of 5.2%. The terminal value represents ~77% of total EV, which i=
s appropriate for a high-quality, low-CapEx business =E2=80=94 similar to e=
nterprise software companies. This is a feature, not a flaw.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">2. How did you calculate WAC=
C, and how would you defend your beta?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">WACC =3D (E=
/V) =C3=97 Ke + (D/V) =C3=97 Kd =C3=97 (1=E2=80=93t). I used a 5-year weekl=
y beta regression of Intertek vs. the FTSE All-Share, producing a levered b=
eta of 0.82. To validate: I unlevered the beta using the Hamada equation (=
=CE=B2U =3D =CE=B2L / [1 + (1-t)(D/E)]), getting =CE=B2U =3D 0.65, and cros=
s-checked this against peer unlevered betas (SGS 0.62, BV 0.64, Eurofins 0.=
68) =E2=80=94 all cluster around 0.62=E2=80=930.68, which validates my esti=
mate. The beta of 0.82 reflects Intertek's quasi-defensive profile (regulat=
ory revenue floor) with modest cyclicality in trade/resources segments. It'=
s well below the FTSE market beta of 1.0, consistent with TIC being a high-=
quality, low-volatility business.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">3. Why does Terminal Value r=
epresent ~77% of Enterprise Value? Should that concern you?</div><div class=
=3D"qa-a">No, it should not concern you =E2=80=94 it's mathematically expec=
ted and actually appropriate here. TV is high as a % of EV for businesses w=
ith: (a) long asset lives =E2=80=94 accreditations, brand, and client relat=
ionships are multi-decade assets; (b) asset-light models =E2=80=94 CapEx is=
 only 3.5% of revenue, so the explicit period FCF is modest relative to the=
 business's intrinsic long-duration value; and (c) stable, growing earnings=
 well past the explicit forecast window. The GGM TV of 8,577m implies an ex=
it multiple of only 10.7=C3=97 EBITDA =E2=80=94 below the current trading m=
ultiple of 11.8=C3=97. That internal consistency check tells me my terminal=
 value is conservative, not aggressive. If a recruiter pushes back, point o=
ut that Microsoft, SAP, and other high-quality compounders all show TV of 7=
0=E2=80=9385% of EV =E2=80=94 and they're considered defensive assets.</div=
></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">4. What's the difference bet=
ween Enterprise Value and Equity Value, and how did you bridge them?</div><=
div class=3D"qa-a">Enterprise Value is the total value of the business avai=
lable to all capital providers =E2=80=94 debt, equity, and quasi-debt holde=
rs. Equity Value is what remains after satisfying all non-equity claims. Th=
e bridge: Equity Value =3D EV =E2=80=93 Net Debt =E2=80=93 Minorities =E2=
=80=93 Pension Deficit =E2=80=93 IFRS 16 Lease Liabilities + Non-operating =
Assets. For Intertek: EV of =C2=A38,749m minus net debt (=C2=A3940m), minor=
ities (=C2=A385m), pension (=C2=A342m), and IFRS 16 lease liabilities (=C2=
=A3215m) =3D Equity Value of =C2=A37,467m. The key technical point is IFRS =
16 =E2=80=94 under IFRS, operating leases are capitalised as financial liab=
ilities and must be included in the EV=E2=86=92Equity bridge. This is a ver=
y common interview trap. The EV multiple (EV/EBITDA) is the cleanest compar=
ison across companies because it is capital structure neutral =E2=80=94 it =
doesn't matter whether a company is financed with debt or equity.</div></di=
v>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">5. Why did you choose a 13=
=C3=97 exit multiple for your terminal value?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">I ch=
ose 13=C3=97 EV/EBITDA because: (1) it's a modest discount to the current N=
TM sector median of 13.5=C3=97 (SGS, BV, Eurofins average); (2) it implies =
minimal multiple expansion from Intertek's current 11.8=C3=97 =E2=80=94 I'm=
 essentially assuming partial convergence to the sector median, not an opti=
mistic re-rating; (3) TIC has traded at 13=E2=80=9316=C3=97 through the cyc=
le, so 13=C3=97 represents mid-cycle, not peak; and (4) the GGM method prov=
ides a sanity check: 13=C3=97 exit multiple implies an implicit growth rate=
 in perpetuity of ~7.3% in the GGM framework (TV / EBITDA =3D (1+g)/(WACC=
=E2=80=93g)), which is higher than my explicit 2.5% assumption =E2=80=94 su=
ggesting the exit multiple may even be conservative. The range of 12=E2=80=
=9314=C3=97 used in sensitivity analysis brackets the reasonable outcomes.<=
/div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">6. How would you calculate s=
ynergies in an M&amp;A transaction, and how do they impact valuation?</div>=
<div class=3D"qa-a">Synergies are additional cash flows generated because t=
wo companies are combined that neither could generate alone. Types: (1) Cos=
t synergies =E2=80=94 headcount, lab consolidation, SG&amp;A reduction (mos=
t achievable; 75=E2=80=93100% realised in 3=E2=80=935Y); (2) Revenue synerg=
ies =E2=80=94 cross-selling, market access (20=E2=80=9350% realised; harder=
 to model); (3) Financial synergies =E2=80=94 tax benefits, lower cost of d=
ebt via credit rating improvement. For Intertek, I estimate =C2=A3125=E2=80=
=93185m annual synergies from a strategic deal =E2=80=94 this has a PV of =
=C2=A3900=E2=80=931,200m at WACC, which justifies a 15=E2=80=9325% control =
premium above the standalone DCF. In practice: acquirer pays up to (standal=
one intrinsic value + PV of synergies =E2=80=93 execution risk discount). A=
ny premium paid above synergy value destroys acquirer shareholder value.</d=
iv></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">7. Why do precedent transact=
ion multiples typically exceed trading multiples?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">=
Three main reasons: (1) Control premium =E2=80=94 acquirers pay 20=E2=80=93=
30% above the undisturbed share price to gain control and the right to extr=
act synergies. Public shareholders demand compensation for giving up their =
shares; (2) Synergy value =E2=80=94 the acquirer prices in synergies that i=
ncrease the combined value above the standalone trading value; (3) Competit=
ive M&amp;A process =E2=80=94 formal sale processes with multiple bidders d=
rive prices to the highest marginal bidder's valuation, not the average. Ad=
ditionally, in precedent transactions, acquirers often have private informa=
tion from management presentations that increases their willingness to pay.=
 The control premium for Intertek (at ~25%) adds =C2=A31.3=E2=80=931.5bn to=
 standalone equity value, pushing the price from ~3,320p to ~4,600p in an a=
cquisition scenario.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">8. What's unlevered free cas=
h flow and why do we use it in a DCF rather than net income or levered FCF?=
</div><div class=3D"qa-a">Unlevered FCF (UFCF) =3D EBIT =C3=97 (1=E2=80=93t=
) + D&amp;A =E2=80=93 CapEx =E2=80=93 =CE=94NWC. It represents the cash flo=
w available to all capital providers (debt + equity) as if the business wer=
e unlevered (no interest payments, no tax shield benefit). We use it for th=
ree reasons: (1) Capital structure neutrality =E2=80=94 UFCF allows us to c=
ompare companies regardless of leverage, and to separate the operating valu=
e from financing decisions; (2) Discounting at WACC =E2=80=94 we discount U=
FCF at WACC (which already incorporates the debt tax shield via the after-t=
ax cost of debt), so using levered FCF would double-count the tax shield; (=
3) Enterprise Value output =E2=80=94 UFCF discounted at WACC gives EV direc=
tly, which we then bridge to equity value. Using net income would mix opera=
ting and financing decisions, making cross-company comparisons meaningless.=
</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">9. How do you select compara=
bles, and what are the weaknesses of your comp set?</div><div class=3D"qa-a=
">I select comparables based on: (1) Business similarity =E2=80=94 same sec=
tor (TIC), similar revenue mix, similar end markets; (2) Scale proximity =
=E2=80=94 ideally within 1/3=C3=97 to 3=C3=97 of subject company revenue; (=
3) Geography =E2=80=94 preference for European peers given Intertek's UK li=
sting; (4) Capital structure =E2=80=94 leverage-adjustable metrics (EV/EBIT=
DA) reduce the sensitivity to capital structure differences. Weaknesses of =
my Intertek comp set: (a) SGS and BV are Swiss/French-listed and trade at a=
n EU premium; (b) Eurofins has a different mix (heavily life sciences/pharm=
a testing) with structurally higher margins (22.4%); (c) Element Materials =
is private =E2=80=94 no market data; (d) MISTRAS operates primarily in non-=
destructive testing with lower-quality earnings =E2=80=94 dragging the mult=
iple average down. I weight the core TIC peers (SGS, BV, Eurofins) most hea=
vily and use MISTRAS as a lower bound anchor.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">10. What does your sensitivi=
ty analysis tell you about the conviction in your valuation?</div><div clas=
s=3D"qa-a">The sensitivity analysis tells me: (1) the base case is well-sup=
ported across methodologies; (2) the valuation is most sensitive to WACC an=
d exit multiple (=C2=B11% WACC moves price by ~300=E2=80=93400p); (3) reven=
ue growth and margin assumptions create a wide outcome range but the base c=
ase sits comfortably in the "green zone" of sensible inputs; (4) even under=
 aggressive bear case assumptions (WACC=3D9.5%, exit=3D11=C3=97), the impli=
ed price of ~2,760p represents only ~17% downside from the current ~3,320p =
=E2=80=94 limited absolute loss for a high-quality business. The tight rang=
e of outcomes in the mid-case scenario (3,470=E2=80=933,840p across methodo=
logies) gives me high conviction in the "modestly undervalued" thesis. The =
valuation doesn't rely on heroic assumptions =E2=80=94 the upside comes fro=
m simply trading at sector median multiples.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">11. How would you adjust the=
 DCF if Intertek made a large acquisition?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">For a l=
arge acquisition, I would: (1) Model the acquired entity's standalone reven=
ues, EBITDA, and FCF as a separate overlay on the forecast; (2) Include acq=
uisition synergies (net of integration costs and dis-synergies) with time d=
elays (typically 50% Year 1, 75% Year 2, 100% Year 3); (3) Reflect the cash=
 paid for the acquisition as a Year 0 use of cash (reducing opening equity =
value) or adjust net debt if debt-funded; (4) Update capital structure and =
re-run WACC to reflect any change in leverage; (5) Add goodwill and intangi=
ble amortisation from acquisition accounting. In practice, bolt-on M&amp;A =
at 8=E2=80=9310=C3=97 EBITDA (typical for TIC) is immediately accretive to =
Intertek given its capital-efficient model =E2=80=94 this is a bull case ca=
talyst I've noted but not embedded in the base case (to stay conservative).=
</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">12. What is the mid-year con=
vention and why did you use it?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">The mid-year conve=
ntion assumes that free cash flows are generated evenly throughout the year=
 and therefore should be discounted as if received at the midpoint of each =
year (0.5, 1.5, 2.5...) rather than at year-end (1, 2, 3...). Without it, w=
e'd implicitly assume all cash is received on December 31 =E2=80=94 unreali=
stic. Using mid-year produces a higher DCF value than year-end convention (=
cash is worth more when received mid-year than at year-end). For Intertek: =
discount factor for Year 1 =3D 1/(1.085)^0.5 =3D 0.961 rather than 1/(1.085=
)^1 =3D 0.922 =E2=80=94 a meaningful 4% difference. Mid-year convention is =
standard in LBO modelling and professional DCF. Always use it unless the bu=
siness generates revenue in lump-sum payments at year-end (e.g., milestone-=
based contracts).</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">13. If EBITDA goes up by =C2=
=A310m, what happens to Enterprise Value?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">A quick =
mental math answer: Enterprise Value =E2=89=88 EBITDA =C3=97 EV/EBITDA mult=
iple. If EBITDA increases by =C2=A310m and we apply the current 11.8=C3=97 =
multiple, EV increases by ~=C2=A3118m. In a DCF context, the impact depends=
 on whether the EBITDA increase is permanent or temporary. If permanent (re=
curring improvement), it will also increase Terminal Value: at a 13=C3=97 e=
xit multiple, an incremental =C2=A310m EBITDA in Year 5 adds =C2=A3130m to =
terminal value, which discounted back at (1.085)^5 adds ~=C2=A386m to EV to=
day. Total impact: ~=C2=A3118m from trading multiples, or ~=C2=A386m purely=
 from the terminal value effect of a permanent margin improvement. In an M&=
amp;A context, buyers will pay for the incremental EBITDA at the acquisitio=
n multiple =E2=80=94 so every =C2=A310m of synergies at 15=C3=97 acquisitio=
n multiple =3D =C2=A3150m of value creation. This is the core synergy valua=
tion logic.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">14. What are the risks to yo=
ur thesis and what would make you change your recommendation?</div><div cla=
ss=3D"qa-a">Key risks: (1) Sterling appreciation =E2=80=94 70%+ of Intertek=
's revenues are non-GBP; a 10% GBP appreciation creates ~=C2=A3200m of tran=
slation headwinds; (2) Industrial recession =E2=80=94 resources/trade segme=
nts (~40% of revenue) have modest cyclicality; (3) Regulatory simplificatio=
n =E2=80=94 unlikely but a structural tail risk; (4) Management quality det=
erioration on capital allocation (overpaying for M&amp;A). I would change m=
y recommendation to HOLD/SELL if: (a) organic revenue growth decelerates be=
low 3% on a trailing 12-month basis without a credible recovery catalyst; (=
b) EBITDA margin contracts by more than 150bps persistently; (c) management=
 announces a transformational acquisition at &gt;18=C3=97 EBITDA without cl=
ear synergy justification; (d) WACC rises sustainably above 9.5% (i.e., UK =
rates remain elevated structurally). A formal takeover approach or strategi=
c review would be the bull case trigger to upgrade to strong BUY with a 25=
=E2=80=9330% premium target.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">15. Explain the difference b=
etween EV/EBITDA and P/E. Which do you prefer and why?</div><div class=3D"q=
a-a">EV/EBITDA =3D Enterprise Value / EBITDA. Capital structure-neutral: co=
mpares value of the whole enterprise (debt + equity) against pre-interest, =
pre-tax earnings. P/E =3D Market Cap / Net Earnings. Equity-level metric: l=
evered, after-tax, after-interest. I prefer EV/EBITDA for cross-company com=
parisons because: (1) it neutralises capital structure differences =E2=80=
=94 a 5=C3=97 levered company would have very different P/E from an unlever=
ed peer, even with identical operating performance; (2) it's unaffected by =
different accounting treatments for interest and taxes across jurisdictions=
; (3) it normalises for D&amp;A differences arising from varying CapEx regi=
mes and acquisition accounting. P/E is useful for: (1) equity investor pers=
pective; (2) comparing companies within the same accounting/tax jurisdictio=
n; (3) quick valuation anchor for liquid large-caps. For Intertek (UK-liste=
d, IFRS, moderate leverage), both are informative =E2=80=94 EV/EBITDA for p=
eer comparison, P/E for absolute historical context.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">16. What's the difference be=
tween EBITDA and Free Cash Flow? Why does FCF conversion matter?</div><div =
class=3D"qa-a">EBITDA is an accounting measure of operating earnings before=
 non-cash charges (D&amp;A) and below-the-line items. It's useful as a prox=
y for cash earnings but ignores CapEx, working capital movements, and taxes=
. Free Cash Flow (UFCF or levered FCF) adjusts EBITDA for: (1) cash taxes p=
aid; (2) CapEx (real cash investment required to sustain the business); (3)=
 changes in working capital (cash tied up or released by the business cycle=
). FCF conversion =3D FCF / EBITDA. Intertek's ~72% conversion is excellent=
 for an industrials business (average industrials: 50=E2=80=9360%). High co=
nversion reflects: low CapEx intensity (3.5% of revenue), modest working ca=
pital requirements (professional services collect receivables relatively qu=
ickly), and low effective tax rate. FCF conversion is critical because EBIT=
DA can be manipulated by accounting choices; FCF is much harder to inflate =
sustainably. Importantly, an acquirer pays based on future FCF, not EBITDA =
=E2=80=94 so the conversion ratio directly impacts acquisition economics.</=
div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">17. How would you value a co=
mpany with negative EBITDA?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">Several approaches for=
 negative-EBITDA companies: (1) Revenue multiples (EV/Revenue) =E2=80=94 ap=
propriate when revenue is growing rapidly and EBITDA is intentionally suppr=
essed (e.g., early-stage SaaS); (2) Forward-looking DCF to profitability =
=E2=80=94 model the path to break-even and discount long-dated cash flows; =
(3) Gross margin multiples =E2=80=94 EV/Gross Profit, useful when the busin=
ess has positive gross margins but high SG&amp;A/R&amp;D; (4) NAV-based =E2=
=80=94 for asset-heavy or pre-revenue businesses; (5) Precedent transaction=
s/funding rounds =E2=80=94 particularly for tech startups. The key is ident=
ifying which metric most closely approximates the economic engine of the bu=
siness. For Intertek, this is not relevant =E2=80=94 the company is highly =
profitable =E2=80=94 but if a PE sponsor were considering acquiring a loss-=
making specialty testing startup as a bolt-on, they'd use EV/Revenue multip=
les anchored to comparable public TIC/software businesses with similar grow=
th profiles.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">18. What's a leveraged buyou=
t, and is Intertek a good LBO candidate?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">An LBO is=
 a transaction where a financial sponsor acquires a company using significa=
nt debt (typically 50=E2=80=9370% of the acquisition price), with the expec=
tation that the target's FCF will service the debt over a 3=E2=80=937 year =
hold period, and the sponsor will exit at a multiple expansion with an impl=
ied equity IRR of 20%+. Good LBO characteristics: (1) stable, predictable F=
CF for debt servicing; (2) asset-light for collateral-light financing; (3) =
multiple expansion opportunity; (4) operational improvement levers. Interte=
k partially fits: strong FCF (&gt;85% conversion), asset-light, regulatory =
moat. However, at 11.8=C3=97 EV/EBITDA entry, a standard PE deal structure =
(5=C3=97 leverage) produces only ~13=E2=80=9315% IRR over a 5Y hold =E2=80=
=94 below the 20% hurdle for most PE firms. Intertek is better suited to a =
strategic acquirer (who can justify a higher price via synergies) or a long=
-hold PE strategy (7=E2=80=9310 years) that captures multiple expansion plu=
s bolt-on M&amp;A value creation.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">19. What's the risk-free rat=
e and why use the 10-year gilt specifically?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">The r=
isk-free rate is the theoretical return on an investment with zero default =
risk and zero reinvestment risk. In practice, government bond yields of the=
 same currency as the cash flows being discounted are used as proxies. I us=
e the 10-year UK Gilt (4.15% as of December 2024) because: (1) Intertek rep=
orts in GBP and the majority of its value is denominated in GBP-equivalent =
flows; (2) 10 years matches the typical DCF forecast horizon (5-year explic=
it + 5-year terminal fade); (3) UK Gilts are the closest available instrume=
nt to a risk-free asset in sterling. I do not use a short-term gilt (too se=
nsitive to near-term monetary policy) or a 30-year gilt (too exposed to lon=
g-duration inflation risk). If Intertek earned primarily USD revenues, I'd =
use the US 10-year Treasury instead. The risk-free rate choice directly anc=
hors the CAPM cost of equity =E2=80=94 every 50bps change in Rf changes WAC=
C by ~37bps (given 74% equity weight and beta of 0.82), which moves DCF val=
ue by ~4=E2=80=935%.</div></div>

    <div class=3D"qa-item"><div class=3D"qa-q">20. You said Intertek is mod=
estly undervalued. What's your strongest argument, and what would a bear sa=
y?</div><div class=3D"qa-a">Strongest bull argument: Intertek trades at a ~=
8=E2=80=9310% discount to its closest TIC peers (SGS, Bureau Veritas, Eurof=
ins) on EV/EBITDA despite comparable organic growth rates and similar EBITD=
A margins. The discount reflects UK listing geography and perceived macro s=
ensitivity =E2=80=94 not fundamental operational inferiority. As UK-specifi=
c macro headwinds recede and Intertek's ESG/EV testing business scales, thi=
s discount should close. Simple convergence to sector median multiple (no h=
eroic assumptions) implies 22=E2=80=9328% share price upside. Bull case =E2=
=80=94 strategic acquisition at 25% premium =E2=80=94 implies 39=E2=80=9345=
% upside. Bear's best argument: The discount is rational, not a mispricing.=
 Eurofins operates in structurally higher-margin life sciences; SGS and BV =
are European and benefit from EU valuation premia; Intertek's trade/resourc=
es segment (40% of revenue) is structurally more cyclical and under secular=
 pressure from supply chain simplification. FCF conversion could deteriorat=
e as CapEx intensity rises to support lab expansion. WACC could rise if UK =
rates stay elevated. The bear says: 11.8=C3=97 is fair value, not a discoun=
t =E2=80=94 Intertek is a good but not great business at a fair price. Coun=
ter: ROIC of 22.4% vs. WACC of 8.5% =3D massive economic value creation. A =
22.4% ROIC business does not trade at 11.8=C3=97 EBITDA in efficient market=
s =E2=80=94 the discount will close.</div></div>

